<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yamada Nagamasa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1590–<a href="/years/1630">1630</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yamada Nagamasa was a Japanese adventurer who settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> in the early seventeenth century, led the Japanese community and its volunteer soldiers in the service of the Siamese kings, and won high rank at court. Drawn into the succession struggles that brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prasat-thong">Prasat Thong</a> to power, he was sent to govern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nakhon-si-thammarat">Nakhon Si Thammarat</a> in the south, where he died in 1630, reportedly poisoned.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yamada-nagamasa.jpg" alt="Yamada Nagamasa" width="320" height="330" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yamada-Nagamasa-Portrait-Shizuoka-Sengen-Shrine.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Yamada Nagamasa was born in Numazu, in Shizuoka, and is said to have been a palanquin bearer for the lord of Numazu. He took part in Japanese trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a> in the period of the red seal ships and settled in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya Kingdom</a> around 1612. From 1617 he headed Ban Yipun, the Japanese village within Ayutthaya, home to roughly 1,000 people: traders, Christians who had fled the persecutions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ronin">rōnin</a> from the losing side at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Battle of Sekigahara</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-osaka">Siege of Osaka</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The settlement exported deer hide to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in exchange for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, swords, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lacquerware">lacquerware</a> and paper, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> noted that its traders challenged the monopoly of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a>. Its men were organised as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">Department</a> of Japanese Volunteers in the king's service. In 15 years Yamada rose from the low rank of Khun to that of Okya, with the title Okya Senaphimuk, and led a Japanese army under the Japanese flag in the campaigns of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songtham">Songtham</a>. He is also alleged to have raided Dutch ships around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavia">Batavia</a> from 1620.</p><p>In 1624 he sailed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and sold a cargo of deer hide in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagasaki">Nagasaki</a>, staying three years in a vain attempt to obtain a red seal permit. In 1626 he gave a temple in his home town a painting of one of his warships; a copy survives, showing Western-style rigging, 18 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannons</a> and sailors in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> gear. In 1628 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> seized one of his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> ships and released it, unwilling to offend the King of Siam.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1630 he was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nakhon-si-thammarat">Ligor</a>, now Nakhon Si Thammarat, with 300 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prasat-thong">Prasat Thong</a>, who had killed King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songtham">Songtham</a>'s sons on his way to the throne, faced Yamada's rebellion and reportedly had him poisoned by Praya Chaiya. The new king then sent 4,000 soldiers against the Japanese settlement, killing 500 Japanese and destroying it, though some 300 to 400 returned in 1633. From 1634 the shōgun issued no more red seal permits for Siam, and a Siamese embassy of 1636 was rejected. Yamada is buried in his home town, and a statue of him stands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yamada Nagamasa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Foreign specialists added firearms and expertise: Portuguese gunners served the kings from the sixteenth century, and a Japanese volunteer corps under <b>Yamada Nagamasa</b> fought for the court in the early seventeenth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The kings hired foreign mercenaries, and foreigners, lacking ties within the kingdom, often rose high at court: <b>Yamada Nagamasa</b>’s Japanese served as King Songtham’s royal guard.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ayutthaya">Social Hierarchy of the Ayutthaya Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Numazu, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. Died in Phra Nakhon Si Ayutthaya, in what is now <a href="/countries/thailand">Thailand</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.1,138.86&amp;year=1616" aria-label="Numazu on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8068 894 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Yamada Nagamasa"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8518 1164h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8518 1164h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35.1,138.86&amp;year=1616">What was here in 1616? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
